user = User(username=username, email=email, password_hash=password_hash)
db.session.add(user)
db.session.commit()
```

### Login template with CSRF and input length limits

```html
<form method="POST" action="{{ url_for('login') }}">
  {{ form.hidden_tag() }}

  <div class="mb-3">
    {{ form.username.label(class="form-label") }}
    {{ form.username(
        class="form-control",
        maxlength="50",
        autocomplete="username"
    ) }}
  </div>

  <div class="mb-3">
    {{ form.password.label(class="form-label") }}
    {{ form.password(
        class="form-control",
        maxlength="50",
        autocomplete="current-password"
    ) }}
  </div>

  {{ form.submit(class="btn btn-primary") }}
</form>
```

### Security notes

- Use an environment variable for `SECRET_KEY`; do not keep `SECRET_KEY = "my_secret_key"`.
- Keep `WTF_CSRF_ENABLED=True` and render `{{ form.hidden_tag() }}`.
- Use HTTPS in production before enabling `SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=True`.
- Verify against `user.password_hash`; do not re-hash the submitted password during login.
- Enforce the same backend limit as the HTML `maxlength="50"` and test 10,000-character inputs to ensure they fail cleanly.
- Apply the Redis lockout after 5 failed attempts for one hour, clear counters on success, and handle Redis connectivity errors without crashing login.
- Use `@login_required` for protected pages and check resource ownership for transactions.
- Avoid `app.run(debug=True)` outside local development.

Use **lazysizes 5.3.2 via CDN** with your **Bootstrap 5.3.0** grid. It keeps the bundle small and integrates cleanly with Bootstrap cards. Since Bootstrap 5 deprecated `card-deck`, use `row`/`col-*` for the gallery.

### 1. Include lazysizes
```html
<link rel="preload"
      href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/lazysizes/5.3.2/lazysizes.min.js"
      as="script">
<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/lazysizes/5.3.2/lazysizes.min.js" async></script>
```

### 2. Use Bootstrap grid + lazy image markup
```html
<div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-2 row-cols-lg-4 g-4" id="project-gallery">
  <div class="col">
    <div class="card h-100">
      <img
        class="card-img-top gallery-image lazy"
        src="placeholder.jpg"
        data-src="images/project-1.jpg"
        alt="Screenshot of Project 1 dashboard"
      >
      <div class="card-body">
        <h5 class="card-title">Project 1</h5>
        <p class="card-text">Project description.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <!-- repeat for each gallery item -->
</div>
```

Key points:
- Put the **real URL** in `data-src`.
- Put a **small low-resolution placeholder** in `src` to avoid layout shifts.
- Add a descriptive `alt` attribute for SEO/accessibility.
- Use Bootstrap classes like `card-img-top`, `row`, `col-*`, and `g-4`/`mb-4`.

### 3. Configure/revalidate lazysizes
If you’re using the same setup as before, configure classes and revalidate after DOM load:

```js
const lazyLoadInstance = lazysizes.config({
  loadingClass: 'ls-loading',
  loadedClass: 'ls-loaded',
  lazyClass: 'lazy',
  errorClass: 'ls-error'
});

lazyLoadInstance.revalidate();

document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  lazyLoadInstance.revalidate();
});
```

If you’re converting existing images programmatically:

```js
const galleryImages = document.querySelectorAll('.gallery-image');

galleryImages.forEach((image) => {
  image.classList.add('lazy');
  if (!image.dataset.src) {
    image.setAttribute('data-src', image.src);
  }
  image.src = 'placeholder.jpg';
  image.removeAttribute('srcset'); // prevents premature loading
});

lazyLoadInstance.revalidate();
```

### 4. Add error handling
```js
galleryImages.forEach((image) => {
  image.addEventListener('error', () => {
    console.error(`Failed to load ${image.src}.`);
    image.src = 'fallback.jpg';
    image.classList.remove('ls-error');
  });
});
```

### 5. Performance/accessibility notes
- Keep the placeholder small to reduce initial load and prevent layout shift.
- Don’t lazy-load above-the-fold hero/LCP images; lazy-load below-the-fold gallery images.
- Use `alt` text on every gallery image for SEO and accessibility.
- If using responsive images, the lazysizes **parent-fit plugin** can help.
- If images are inside Bootstrap modals, keep the real image URL in `data-src` and load it only when the modal opens, e.g. on `shown.bs.modal`. Also use Bootstrap 5 attributes such as `data-bs-toggle` and `data-bs-target`, not `data-toggle`/`data-target`.

If you want the absolutely simplest option, native `<img loading="lazy" alt="...">` works too, but for your lightweight lazysizes-based setup, the above is the smoothest fit with Bootstrap 5.3.0.
